A||N||1||Will||question for lawrence: firebird rotors?||||1||
Z||000000||Will||01-18-2001||10:56 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>I used rear rotors from a 97 Firebird. And they're cheap, too. ~$20 each. You can see a picture or two in this article:<BR> <A HREF="http://www.webelectricmagazine.com/99/4/brakes.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.webelectricmagazine.com/99/4/brakes.htm</A>  <BR>Good luck,<P>-L <A HREF="http://www.webelectricmagazine.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.webelectricmagazine.com</A>  <BR><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Do F-bodies use the 5 on 100mm bolt circle? I was sure they were 5 on 115.<BR>I guess you already did the homework and the offset is the same as the stock rotor?||||reg||1
Z||000001||lawrence||01-18-2001||12:31 PM||||No, the hole pattern on the Firebird rotors is not the same as the Fiero.  I don't know what it is.  I drilled new holes in the spaces between the normal holes.  There is a lot of material there, so there is no issue with strength.<P>The offset is bit more than the Fiero, which helps with the alignment of the adapter bracket.  Too bad there wasn't more offset, because then the adapter could have been a simple flat plate.<P>Good luck,<P>-L<BR> <A HREF="http://www.webelectricmagazine.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.webelectricmagazine.com</A> <BR> <A HREF="http://members.nccw.net/webe/products" TARGET=_blank>http://members.nccw.net/webe/products</A>  - Products Page||||reg||1
